From 75e9f574b695ff61b5e6e4ae82f4420bff787138 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:09:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: profile the recording medium and tune the writers and
 the kernel for it

Core knew its drive by a volume label. Workers, queue depth, direct I/O and
the camera buffer count depended on the recording format only; nothing in
the repo touched the block queue or vm.dirty_*; Core's own mounts carried no
noatime. Cinemate classifies the medium twice (connection for kernel knobs,
filesystem for the writer topology) and applies the kernel side only to the
recording target. Logic ported, in Core's shape:

- util/MediaProfile.hpp: connection class from /sys/class/block/<root>/device
  (realpath + uevent: pi-nvme, usb-uas, usb-storage, sd-card, other, unknown;
  no fallback that cannot say no), filesystem from the mount table (ext4,
  exfat, vfat, ntfs, ntfs-fuse, other), a writer table per filesystem
  (uncompressed cDNG workers 4/2, queue 12/16, +2 raw camera buffers on
  FAT-family and NTFS, direct I/O off on FUSE), summary string. Pure parts
  host-tested (KB tools/media_profile_test.cpp).
- DngRecordingQueue takes the profile before start: worker count
  (APERTAR_CDNG_UNCOMPRESSED_WORKERS still wins), queue depth, the encoder's
  direct I/O preference (APERTAR_CDNG_DIRECT_IO still wins); the clip line
  gains media_class= media_fs= direct_io=.
- rawCameraBufferCount adds the profile's extra buffers for the configured
  media root (APERTAR_RAW_CAMERA_BUFFERS still wins).
- The media watcher logs the profile on every medium change, publishes
  media_class/media_profile in /api/state, and asks the root helper
  /usr/local/libexec/apertar-media-tune (scripts/, sudoers and tmpfiles
  snippets in config/) to apply block-queue and vm.dirty_* settings for the
  mounted recording drive and restore them when it goes; without the helper
  one journal line says so. APERTAR_MEDIA_TUNE=0 disables.
- mount_media adds noatime (ext4 too).

Starting points for T29 to measure, not Cinemate's numbers as facts.
Host-checked: ApertarCoreApp.cpp and CdngEncoder.cpp pass g++ -fsyntax-only;
CameraController.cpp needs libcamera and is not compiled here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T93jucKMhWYnQM1yyFVkbH
